WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:12.965
In the last video, we got to know the basic concepts of probability theory, ending with the random variable. In this video, we look at further concepts and tools for working with random variables.

2
00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:17.546
The learning goals of this video are:

3
00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:22.642
First: to distinguish between discrete and continuous random variables.

4
00:00:23.042 --> 00:00:27.373
Second: to distinguish between a random variable and its realisation.

5
00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:37.276
And third: to set up the probability mass function and the cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable, and to represent them graphically.

6
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.718
We continue with the example from the last video: we roll two dice, a red one and a green one.

7
00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:55.219
The random variable X gives the sum of the two numbers. If the red die shows two and the green die shows five, X equals seven.

8
00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:01.547
The grid shows once again, for each of the thirty-six outcomes, the corresponding value of X.

9
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:13.130
We now turn to some new concepts for random variables. There are two types of random variables: discrete and continuous.

10
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.187
For a discrete random variable, we can list in advance which values it can take.

11
00:01:18.687 --> 00:01:31.819
An example: X describes a count, such as the number of cancellations at a hotel on one day. Possible values are zero, one, two, three and so on, in this case up to the total number of bookings as an upper limit.

12
00:01:32.419 --> 00:01:42.693
A continuous random variable, on the other hand, takes values with infinitely many decimal places. Whole intervals of the number line are possible, or even the entire number line.

13
00:01:43.193 --> 00:01:56.628
An example: X describes a distance or a duration that can be measured very precisely, such as the travel time to university. It might be 23.47 minutes, for instance, and a more precise measurement adds further decimal places.

14
00:01:57.228 --> 00:02:08.246
In our example, the sum X is a discrete random variable, because its possible values can be listed in advance: two, three, four and so on, up to twelve.

15
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:19.867
Next, we clarify a key point about perspective. For any random experiment, we can distinguish between the ex-ante and the ex-post perspective.

16
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:38.188
Ex ante means: before the random experiment is carried out. From this perspective, we write random variables with capital letters, here capital X. At this point, we know which values X can take, and we can talk about probabilities, for example the probability that the sum is exactly seven.

17
00:02:38.738 --> 00:02:47.645
Ex post means: after it has been carried out. Now we can observe which value the random variable has produced, for example which sum was rolled.

18
00:02:48.195 --> 00:02:57.307
We call this observed value the realisation. For the realisation, we use the corresponding lower-case letter, here lower-case x.

19
00:02:57.857 --> 00:03:06.504
An example: the red die shows three and the green die shows four. The realisation of the sum is then lower-case x equals seven.

20
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:16.065
For the sum, we now list all possible realisations in a table. They are the whole numbers from two to twelve.

21
00:03:16.615 --> 00:03:29.384
For each realisation, we add the probability that X takes exactly this value. We determine it with the Laplace approach: the number of favourable outcomes divided by the number of possible outcomes.

22
00:03:29.934 --> 00:03:40.991
A sum of two only occurs with the outcome one, one. That is one of thirty-six equally likely outcomes. So, P of X equals two is one thirty-sixth.

23
00:03:41.591 --> 00:03:53.946
A sum of three occurs with two outcomes: one, two and two, one. So, P of X equals three is two thirty-sixths. We keep all fractions over thirty-six, which makes the values easier to compare.

24
00:03:54.546 --> 00:04:06.479
In the same way, we add the remaining values: a sum of four occurs with three outcomes, a sum of five with four, a sum of six with five and a sum of seven with six outcomes.

25
00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:18.849
After that, the number of outcomes decreases again: eight occurs with five outcomes, nine with four, ten with three, eleven with two, and twelve only with six, six.

26
00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:32.001
The table shown here is called the probability mass function, or PMF for short. For each possible realisation, it gives the probability that X takes exactly this value.

27
00:04:32.551 --> 00:04:37.891
In symbols, we write: f of x equals P of X equals x.

28
00:04:38.491 --> 00:04:46.520
This approach is useful for discrete random variables in particular, because their realisations can be listed in advance.

29
00:04:48.620 --> 00:05:08.237
Besides the probability mass function, there is a second way to express the probabilities of a random variable: the cumulative distribution function, or CDF. We add it as a third column. While the probability mass function is written as lower-case f of x, the cumulative distribution function is denoted by capital F of x.

30
00:05:08.787 --> 00:05:19.457
The probability mass function gives the probability that a particular value is taken exactly. The cumulative distribution function gives the probability that a particular value is not exceeded.

31
00:05:20.057 --> 00:05:27.573
In the first row, x equals two, the PMF and the CDF still have the same value, since a sum below two cannot occur.

32
00:05:28.173 --> 00:05:45.964
In the next row, we look at x equals three. The CDF now gives the probability that the sum is at most three. This covers the sums two and three. So we add the corresponding probabilities: one thirty-sixth plus two thirty-sixths gives three thirty-sixths.

33
00:05:46.564 --> 00:06:08.158
For x equals four, the probability of a sum of four is added. The probability of rolling at most four is the probability of at most three plus the probability of exactly four. In numbers: three thirty-sixths plus three thirty-sixths gives six thirty-sixths. So from row to row, the CDF grows by the corresponding probability from the PMF.

34
00:06:08.758 --> 00:06:12.698
In this way, we fill in the third column step by step.

35
00:06:17.198 --> 00:06:25.260
In the last row, we get thirty-six thirty-sixths, that is, one or 100 per cent: a sum of at most twelve is certain.

36
00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.655
Finally, we represent the probability mass function of the sum graphically.

37
00:06:32.205 --> 00:06:43.491
The horizontal axis shows the possible realisations x from two to twelve, and the vertical axis shows f of x, that is, the probability that the sum takes exactly this value.

38
00:06:44.041 --> 00:06:49.415
Above each value, we draw a bar whose height equals the probability from the table.

39
00:06:50.015 --> 00:07:01.202
The largest single probability belongs to a sum of seven, with six thirty-sixths, or just under 17 per cent. Towards both ends, the probabilities become smaller.

40
00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:08.225
To sum up: random variables can be discrete or continuous.

41
00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:19.011
Before the random experiment is carried out, we consider the random variable, capital X. Afterwards, we observe a realisation, lower-case x.

42
00:07:19.611 --> 00:07:31.297
For discrete random variables, the probability mass function gives the probability of exactly one value. The cumulative distribution function gives the probability that a given value is not exceeded.
